  "editorReview": {
    "text": "This is a work of formidable archival reach and structural ambition, tracking the military, scientific, and human threads of the 1946 atomic tests with a panoramic rigor. Its power lies in specific, devastating juxtapositions—like the confident press release placed against the grim radiological log—and in grounded sensory details that make history tangible. However, the reading experience is periodically fractured by a jarring mechanical repetition of key phrases and paragraphs, which stalls the narrative momentum it seeks to build. The argument about institutional denial is compelling, but th",
